Taking the dot product allows us to calculate the projection of $\begin{pmatrix} 6-2t \\ 3-6t \\ 3-3t \end{pmatrix}$ onto $\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix}.$  We want this projection to be equal to our direction vector, so this dot product is again equal to
\[\begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} = 4.
\begin{pmatrix} 6-2t \\ 3-6t \\ 3-3t \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 6 \\ -3 \end{pmatrix} = 4(6-2t)(-2) + 4(3-6t)(6) + 4(3-3t)(-3) = 108 - 72t.
\]This gives us the equation $4(6-2t)(-2) + 4(3

-6t)(6) + 4(3-3t)(-3) = 4.$ Solving, we find $t = \frac{9}{19}.$  Hence, the point we seek is
\[\boxed{\left( \frac{40}{19}, \frac{54}{19}, \frac{4}{19} \right)}.\]

